{ignore}
<p><code>{timeago $created}</code> writes how long ago the moment was - just now, 5 minutes ago,
yesterday, 3 weeks ago, and for a moment still to come, in 2 days - inside a
<code>&lt;time class="pad-timeago"&gt;</code>. The element's <code>datetime</code> is the moment in
ISO 8601, for machines, and its <code>title</code> the moment in full, which a browser shows on
hover. The words are those of the <code>ago</code> pipe, with the semantic element around
them.</p>

<p>Now is <code>padNow ()</code>, so <code>padNowFreeze ()</code> in PHP holds a page still for
a test. <code>now=</code> counts from a given moment instead - which is how the examples here stay
the same on every request. <code>format=</code> is the PHP date format of the title.</p>

<p>An empty moment writes nothing, so a row without a date leaves no empty element behind. A
moment, or a <code>now=</code>, that cannot be read as a date is a strict error. For the time
left until a moment, use <a href="?tag/countdown"><code>&#123;countdown&#125;</code></a>.</p>
{/ignore}
